          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I get chest discomfort only when I am anxious, never on exertion. How do I tell the difference, and what does guidance describe about assessing it?",
          "answer": "**If you have chest discomfort right now, or it comes on with any of the signs listed below, call emergency services now. Do not drive yourself.** Chest discomfort can be a sign of a heart attack, and the guidance says this cannot be ruled out by how the pain feels (*Chest pain that may indicate a heart attack*).\n\n**Anxiety does not rule out a heart cause.** The guidance does not treat anxiety-linked chest pain as safe by default. A first panic-type episode with chest pain, breathlessness or collapse is treated as a physical emergency until a heart and lung cause has been excluded. A person with an anxiety diagnosis can still have a heart event (*Generalized anxiety disorder*). The sources also say heartburn and cardiac pain cannot be reliably told apart by how they feel, and that relief from antacids does not rule out a heart cause (*GERD*; *Chest pain that may indicate a heart attack*).\n\n**Your \"never on exertion\" detail needs care.** Stable angina is classically brought on by exertion or emotional stress, and it is reproducible: the same trigger brings it on, and rest relieves it within minutes (*Coronary artery disease*). So a link with emotional triggers does not by itself exclude a heart cause. Heart symptoms can also look atypical, especially in women, older adults and people with diabetes (*Coronary artery disease*).\n\n**Red flags that need urgent or emergency care:**\n- Discomfort that is new, unfamiliar, or getting worse\n- Breathlessness, sweating, nausea or unusual tiredness with the discomfort\n- Pain or pressure spreading to the arm, neck, jaw or back\n- Sudden, tearing chest or back pain, or chest pain with fainting or collapse\n- Breathlessness at rest, or difficulty speaking in full sentences\n- Blue or gray lips, face or fingertips\n- Chest pain alongside risk factors such as high blood pressure, diabetes or a family history of early heart disease\n\n**What the guidance says about assessment.** Discomfort is assessed for cardiac causes first, especially when it occurs with exertion, breathlessness, sweating or cardiovascular risk factors (*GERD*). Repeated or new episodes need a clinician to assess them rather than being labelled as anxiety on your own judgement. The sources do not give a checklist that separates anxiety from a cardiac cause, and they do not grade how strong the evidence is for anxiety as the cause once cardiac causes are excluded. My search for more detail on differentiating patterns was not completed, so this answer covers only these passages.\n\n**Where authorities differ.** The retrieved material does not show a documented disagreement on this question.\n\n**Next step.** Ask a doctor to assess the discomfort, including whether it could be heart-related. Bring a note of when it happens, how long it lasts, what seems to set it off and what helps. This matters most if you have risk factors such as high blood pressure, diabetes, smoking or a family history of heart disease. Do not rely on the pattern alone to decide it is only anxiety.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
